@page "/learn/browser-options"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Reconnection settings in C# · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Reconnection settings in C#</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Your scanners lose Wi-Fi for a few seconds. How do you make Blazor retry every 2 seconds?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Interactive Server · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> <code>BrowserOptions</code>, Blazor's start-up settings as a typed C# object.
        In .NET 10 you could set the same reconnection options, but only in JavaScript, by starting Blazor yourself
        with <code>Blazor.start()</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a warehouse pick list. The list is interactive: it runs on the server, over a live connection. When the connection
        drops, Blazor tries to reconnect. By default it tries 30 times: 10 straight away, then 10 five seconds apart,
        then 10 thirty seconds apart. Scanners that drop out for a few seconds need something steadier: a try every 2
        seconds, 20 times.
    </p>
    <p>
        This page asks for that the .NET 10 way, in a hand-written <code>Blazor.start()</code> script. It gets two
        option names slightly wrong, and nothing catches it. A second version, <b>Settings from config</b>, spells
        them right and reads the values from configuration, but one of the values is missing from config.
    </p>
    <p>
        A yellow banner appears above the list when the connection drops. It counts the tries and times the waits
        between them.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="Settings in a Blazor.start() script" Url="demo/browser-options/pick-list/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: Blazor.start() in JavaScript" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Press <b>Pick</b> on a line, then <b>Drop my Wi-Fi for 10 seconds</b>. The banner jumps to "11 of 30": ten
            tries went at once, and the next waited 5 seconds. Those are Blazor's defaults, and nothing warned you.
            <b>Sent to the browser</b> shows the script with the wrong names.
            Then choose <b>Settings from config</b>. <b>Sent to the browser</b> shows <code>maxRetries: ,</code> with
            no number. Blazor didn't start, so <b>Pick</b> does nothing.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>Nothing checks a JavaScript object</h2>
    <p>Here's what the page sent, beside the names Blazor reads:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The script, and what Blazor made of it</span>
        <pre class="snip">Blazor.start({ circuit: { reconnectionOptions: {
    <span class="err">maxRetry</span>: 20,            <span class="dim">// Blazor reads maxRetries</span>
    <span class="err">retryIntervalMs</span>: 2000     <span class="dim">// Blazor reads retryIntervalMilliseconds</span>
} } });                    <span class="err">both ignored: the defaults apply</span>

<span class="dim">// Settings from config: Razor pastes in a value nobody set</span>
maxRetries: ,              <span class="err">SyntaxError: Blazor never starts</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The options are a plain JavaScript object. A misspelt name is just an extra property that nothing reads. And
        because the page starts Blazor itself, a script with a syntax error means Blazor never starts. The page looks
        fine until someone clicks.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Put the settings in C#</h2>
    <p>Build a <code>BrowserOptions</code> and pass it to the page with <code>&lt;ConfigureBrowser&gt;</code>.</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="hl">&lt;ConfigureBrowser Options="warehouseWifi" /&gt;</span>

static readonly BrowserOptions warehouseWifi = new()
{
    InteractiveServer =
    {
        <span class="hl">ReconnectionMaxRetries = 20,</span>
        <span class="hl">ReconnectionRetryInterval = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2),</span>
    },
};</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        Now a misspelt name won't compile. A value you leave out is <code>null</code>, which keeps Blazor's default.
        You don't write <code>Blazor.start()</code> at all.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same pick list, settings in C#" Url="demo/browser-options/pick-list"
                Label=".NET 11: BrowserOptions in C#">
        <Caption>
            Press <b>Pick</b> on a line, then <b>Drop my Wi-Fi for 10 seconds</b>. The banner reads "Rejoin attempt 1
            of 20", and it measures a 2-second wait before each try. After 10 seconds it
            reconnects, and your pick is still there. <b>Sent to the browser</b> shows the C# names in camelCase, and
            the <code>TimeSpan</code> in milliseconds.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here are the two pages:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/Reconnect/PickList.razor" Feature="browser-options">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's page. The settings are a <code>static readonly</code> object, built once, and only this page gets
            them.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/Reconnect/PickListNet10.razor" Feature="browser-options">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 1's page, for comparison only, so skim it. It's the whole HTML document, because
            <code>Blazor.start()</code> needs <code>autostart="false"</code> on the script tag. The highlighted block
            is the old way: that script tag, then the two scripts from the box above. The rest, such as the
            <code>From</code> parameter and the in-memory configuration, is just there to run the demo.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Start Blazor yourself</h2>
    <p>We tried three versions of the script on .NET 10, and dropped the connection each time:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Three Blazor.start() scripts in .NET 10, and what the browser did</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">maxRetries: 20,
retryIntervalMilliseconds: 2000</pre>
                <span class="why">"1 of 20, 2 of 20…", trying again in 2 seconds</span>
                <div class="verdict ok">✓ works, if every name is right</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip"><span class="err">maxRetry</span>: 20,
<span class="err">retryIntervalMs</span>: 2000</pre>
                <span class="why">"11 of 30" after a second, then trying again in 5 seconds</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ the defaults, with no warning</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">maxRetries:
  @@Config["Reconnect:Tries"],</pre>
                <span class="why">renders <code>maxRetries: ,</code>: "SyntaxError: Unexpected token ','"</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ Blazor never starts</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        Even the working script had a cost. You had to turn off autostart in <code>App.razor</code>, keep settings in
        JavaScript where nothing checks them, and, if your site has a strict security policy, loosen it to allow a script written into the page.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>Typed settings in C#</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 the same settings are C# properties. Set them for one page with <code>&lt;ConfigureBrowser&gt;</code>,
        as Fig. 2 does, or for every page with <code>WithBrowserOptions</code> in <code>Program.cs</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        Configuration is safe too, because there's no script for Razor to paste a missing value into. Read the value
        as <code>int?</code>: a missing key is <code>null</code>, which keeps Blazor's default. (The demos don't show
        this case.)
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Settings from config, in .NET 11</span>
        <pre class="snip">ReconnectionMaxRetries = <span class="hl">config.GetValue&lt;int?&gt;("Reconnect:Tries")</span>,  <span class="dim">// not set: null, Blazor's default</span></pre>
    </div>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Don't mix the two, at least in RC2.</b> This guide runs on .NET 11 RC2, a release candidate, so this may change. In RC2, once <code>WithBrowserOptions</code> is set, <code>&lt;ConfigureBrowser&gt;</code> writes into the app-wide options. One visit to a page would change every page, for everyone.</li>
        <li><b>Only pages with an interactive component get them.</b> The settings are written just before the page's first Server, WebAssembly or Auto component.</li>
        <li><b>Callbacks still need JavaScript.</b> C# settings can't carry functions. So anything that's code, such as a retry-interval function, <code>configureSignalR</code> or a custom <code>reconnectionHandler</code>, stays in <code>Blazor.start()</code> or in a JS initializer (a script Blazor runs as it starts).</li>
        <li><b>The server's end has its own settings.</b> Blazor's live connection ends at a SignalR hub on the server. To set the hub's options, use <code>AddInteractiveServerRenderMode(o =&gt; o.ConfigureConnection = …)</code>, also new in .NET 11.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
